---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { PrimeHarmonyDate } from "../../lib/calendar";
import SeasonCalendar from "../../components/SeasonCalendar.astro";
import DateConverter from "../../components/DateConverter.astro";
import "../../styles/main.css";
import { createOrdinalFunction } from "../../utils/ordinals";

const today = PrimeHarmonyDate.fromDate(new Date());
const local = today.toLocalDateInfo();

const suffixes = {
  one: "st",
  two: "nd",
  few: "rd",
  other: "th",
};

const toOrdinal = createOrdinalFunction('en', suffixes)

const dayLabel =
  local.season === 0
    ? `${toOrdinal(local.day + 1)} festival day`
    : `${toOrdinal(local.day + 1)} day of Season ${local.season}`;

---

<Layout title="Prime Harmony">
  <main class="container">
    <section class="hero grid">
      <div class="even-column centered">
        <h1>
          {dayLabel}, year {local.year}
        </h1>
        <p>
          Year {local.cycleInfo.year} of {
            toOrdinal(local.cycleInfo.cycle)
          } cycle
        </p>
        <a role="button" href="#what">What is this?</a>
      </div>

      <div class="container">
        <SeasonCalendar
          currentDay={local.day + 1}
          title={local.season === 0
            ? "Solstice festival"
            : `${toOrdinal(local.season)} season`}
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="convert" class="container compact">
      <h2>Date conversion</h2>
      <DateConverter locale="en" />
    </section>


    <section id="what" class="even-column container">
      <h2>What is this?</h2>

      <div class="grid">
        <div>
          <h3>Calendar that is always up-to-date</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Perennial system</li>
            <li>128-year cycles with natural precision</li>
            <li>Seasons of equal length</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="smallcal">
          <SeasonCalendar />
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        <h3>How does it work?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Four 90-day seasons + 5–6 holidays</li>
          <li>Leap year is when year number in the cycle is prime</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Feature Grid (Optional) -->
    <section class="even-column container">
      <div class="grid">
        <article>
          <h4>🔄 Perennial</h4>
          <p>You don't need to replace calendars.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h4>🌍 In harmony with nature</h4>
          <p>
            Seasons are anchored to the winter solstice, and the mean year
            matches the mean tropical year.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h4>📐 Accurate</h4>
          <p>
            Drift from the mean tropical year is less than a day in 50,000 years.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        <article>
          <h4>📅 Cyclical timekeeping</h4>
          <p>Time is a spiral, not a line. Dates repeat each 128 years.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h4>🔥 Solstice festival</h4>
          <p>
            New year is five or six out-of-season days placed around the winter
            solstice.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h4>🔢 Mathematical harmony</h4>
          <p>Not just a calendar but a mathematical spell.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section>
      <article>
        <details name="faq" open>
          <summary>Why no months?</summary>
          <p>
            Each season could be divided into three months, in which case we
            would get the usual 12 months, but there is no need for division, as
            the length of the seasons is the same and is conveniently divided by
            10.
          </p>
        </details>
        <hr />

        <details name="faq">
          <summary>Why no day names?</summary>
          <p>
            If 10-day weeks (decades) are used, there is little need to name the
            days of the week, as the calendar is constant and is always clear
            what day it is by the last digit.
          </p>
        </details>
        <hr />

        <details name="faq">
          <summary>Why are the seasons equal?</summary>
          <p>
            Equal 90-day seasons keep the calendar simple and the mean year
            accurate, but they cannot all begin exactly at an equinox or
            solstice: the real astronomical seasons are slightly unequal.
          </p>
        </details>
        <hr />

        <details name="faq">
          <summary>Is this serious?</summary>
          <ul>
            <li>
              It is a mathematical and design experiment, not a replacement for
              the civil calendar. It arose from an observation: 31 leap years
              per 128 years is the fraction 31/128, which approximates the
              fractional part of the tropical year very closely, and there are
              exactly 31 primes below 128 — hence the rule.
            </li>
            <li>
              Its practical use is as a secondary, seasonal calendar — including
              fantasy worlds and tabletop games: seasons start at the winter
              solstice and the mean year closely matches the tropical year.
            </li>
            <li>
              The math is real: 31 leap years per 128-year cycle, and
              31/128 ≈ 0.24219 is a convergent of the tropical-year fraction.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </details>
      </article>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>
